WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.430
Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous are the Mesozoic periods in order.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.370
Early dinosaur relatives and mammals occurred in Triassic settings.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:13.290
Jurassic dinosaurs diversified, and early birds appeared.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:18.830
Cretaceous communities included varied dinosaurs, birds, flying reptiles and abundant flowering

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:22.150
plants. Marine reptiles and other sea life belong to the same

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.950
history but are not all dinosaurs.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.810
The supplied reconstructions show different land and marine communities.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.910
Dinosaur hip categories, diets and locomotion varied;

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.290
not every dinosaur was a large predator.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.750
Mammals already existed during the Mesozoic, even though their later prominence

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.990
gives the Cenozoic its age-of-mammals label.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.970
Dominance is not the same event as origin.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:50.650
The source links the sixty-six-million-year boundary with the Chicxulub impact in

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.690
the Yucatán region. Dust and gases can reduce incoming sunlight and

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.230
disrupt climate and food webs.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.050
The textbook also discusses Deccan flood-basalt eruptions as a possible contributor.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:06.710
Keep impact evidence, proposed environmental mechanisms and volcanic effects separately identifiable.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:12.970
Cenozoic mammals diversified into many habitats, including ancestors of bats, whales,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.410
primates and hoofed animals. Fossils of bones and teeth document changes

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.330
within lineages, such as horses.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.970
This is a branching history of populations;

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.670
it is not one adult animal transforming into another while the

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:26.630
animation runs.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:32.690
Late Cenozoic ice-age communities included mammoths and other large mammals, many

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:36.757
of which later disappeared. The source places Homo sapiens before three

00:01:36.757 --> 00:01:40.550
hundred thousand years ago and emphasizes that migration details continue to

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.550
change with new evidence. Keep these course statements tied to their

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:47.290
source date rather than presenting them as a permanent complete history.
